# DESCRIPTION

scmi is the agent side of Arm's System Control and Management Interface (SCMI 3.2): on a machine whose firmware owns the power domains, clocks and performance levels, a driver asks for them in messages, and this driver sends them.
devmgr(8) starts it for the SCMI agent its record names, and starts it before every other driver (its class is `power`):

```text
driver scmi
class power
match acpi CIXHA006
```

A message goes through a shared-memory channel (SCMI's §5.1.2): its header and words written to the channel, the channel marked busy, the platform told, and the channel polled, for up to 100 ms, until the platform frees it with its answer.
The agent has one channel for each way of telling the platform that devmgr gave it:

- `mailbox`: the first of its `_DSD`'s `shmem` ranges, told through the doorbell of the first of its `mboxes`, the CIX mailbox's `DB_ACK` register at `0x80`.
- `smc`: the `firmware` range a board rule names, told by an SMC call to the function the rule's SMC resource covers; on the CIX Sky1, TF-A's agent, SiP function `0xc2000001` and shared memory at `0x84380000`.

Each channel is asked its base protocol's version and its vendor at the start.
Power domains go to the `smc` channel where there is one, everything else to the `mailbox`.

devmgr gives the agent's tree to each driver whose device has clocks or SCMI power domains, at `/dev/scmi`; mali(3) powers the Sky1's GPU up through it.

The agent also writes the CPUs' desired-performance registers (ACPI's CPPC) for power(8), which maps no memory itself.
On the Sky1 they are SCMI fast channels in the page of the first mailbox, which only this agent is granted.
devmgr starts the agent with an argument `--cppc=0x<address>/<bits>` for each distinct SystemMemory register that its `cpus` lists, and mounts the agent's tree at `dev/scmi` in its own tree, where power finds it.
The agent writes a register only if the line names an address and a width devmgr listed, the value fits the width, and the register lies wholly inside the page or pages of a range it was granted (its shared memory, mailboxes or firmware range, to the end of the last page), so the check is the register's, not the page's.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/scmi-acpi-_SB_.SCMI` on the Sky1, and at `/dev/scmi` in the drivers that use it.

: `ctl`
  Takes a command a line: `power <domain> on|off` (POWER_STATE_SET, the state on or off with its context lost), `clock <id> on|off` (CLOCK_CONFIG_SET), or `clock <id> rate <hz>` (CLOCK_RATE_SET, synchronous, the platform rounding down, then CLOCK_RATE_GET), or `clock <id> rate` (CLOCK_RATE_GET alone: the rate as it is, shown in the status), numbers in decimal or `0x` hexadecimal.
  `cppc <address> <value> <bits>` stores the value in a CPU's desired-performance register, `bits` being 8, 16, 32 or 64: `cppc 0x659009c 8192 32`.
  A `cppc` line that names a register devmgr didn't list, at another width, with a value too wide for it, or in no page the agent was granted, fails the write as invalid, is counted, and changes nothing.
  A line it can't parse fails the write as invalid; a command the platform refused, or that no channel carries, fails it as an I/O error.
: `status`
  Read only.
  A line for each channel, `mailbox: SCMI 2.0, vendor cix` or `smc c2000001: SCMI 2.0, vendor cix`, or the channel and `FAILED:` and why; `no mailbox: …` where devmgr gave none.
  Then the last state set for each domain and clock, `power 21 on`, `clock 31 on`, and the rate each clock was set to as read back, or read by `clock <id> rate`, `clock 41 rate 148500000`; the last value written to each CPPC register, `cppc 0x659009c 8192 32`, and `cppc writes refused <n>` once one was refused; `commands <n>, failed <n>`; and `last failure: <command>: <why>`, the why `status <n> (<name>)`, `timed out`, `channel error`, `short answer` or `no channel`, or for a refused `cppc` line `not a desired-performance register devmgr listed`, `value too wide` or `in no page the agent was granted`.

